1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:07,066
A I increasingly generates and selects its own training data, through self-rewarding pipelines, constitutional loops, and synthetic data.

2
00:00:07,066 --> 00:00:15,151
A model's current behavior therefore helps determine the data that changes it, so a value can persist, weaken, or amplify through training.

3
00:00:15,151 --> 00:00:21,400
Value dynamics asks how values move once the model sits inside that loop, driving its own training.

4
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:38,674
It borrows the tools of population genetics, because one round of a judging loop is one generation of selection: the candidate answers are a population that varies, the judge keeps some and discards the rest, and training passes what was kept into the model that comes next.

5
00:00:38,674 --> 00:00:49,700
The Price equation tracks how selection changes a population, and the difference in means between the kept candidates and the whole pool is what selection theory calls a selection differential.

6
00:00:50,950 --> 00:00:55,222
I fine-tuned open-weight models with value orientations, risk-seeking and insecure-code-generating.

7
00:00:55,222 --> 00:01:06,330
Each round an organism writes several candidates per prompt, and a judge compares each against an alternative and keeps the ones it prefers as training data.

8
00:01:06,330 --> 00:01:09,320
Held-out prompts re-measure the value between rounds.

9
00:01:09,320 --> 00:01:14,020
Runs varied the judge, the candidate source, and the alternative source.

10
00:01:14,770 --> 00:01:18,505
An organism's value is the mean value score of its answers.

11
00:01:18,505 --> 00:01:23,598
For the gambling organism, the score is the share of answers picking the risky gamble.

12
00:01:23,598 --> 00:01:32,766
For the insecure-code organism, it is how insecure its answers to a few fixed questions about its own coding habits read, scored by its frozen base model.

13
00:01:33,516 --> 00:01:44,608
The breeder's equation relates that selection differential to the response in the next generation, and it names what to measure: variation among the candidates, and which way the judge sorts it.

14
00:01:44,608 --> 00:01:51,048
Spread is the standard deviation of the candidates' value scores within a prompt, averaged over the round's prompts.

15
00:01:51,048 --> 00:01:55,342
Agreement is the correlation between the judge's preferences and those value scores.

16
00:01:55,342 --> 00:02:05,002
Their product forecasts the gap between the kept answers and the pool average, and across every round with logged judge scores it reconstructs the realized gaps closely.

17
00:02:05,752 --> 00:02:08,314
The one-round rule has no fitted coefficient.

18
00:02:08,314 --> 00:02:13,804
The next measured value is the mean value score of the candidates the judge kept.

19
00:02:13,804 --> 00:02:20,026
Holding out each complete experimental condition, it predicts the next value well ahead of assuming no change.

20
00:02:20,776 --> 00:02:25,401
Before selection, the forecast replaces the kept mean with spread times agreement.

21
00:02:25,401 --> 00:02:33,110
On matched rounds that costs a small amount of accuracy, the price of predicting selection rather than watching it happen.

22
00:02:33,110 --> 00:02:40,819
For endpoints, the model repeats that update from the round-one candidate mean, with spread, agreement, and pool composition held fixed.

23
00:02:41,569 --> 00:02:52,214
That iteration predicts the final value of a run from its first round with a mean absolute error of zero point one one eight, against zero point four three one for assuming no change.

24
00:02:52,214 --> 00:02:55,032
Nothing measured after the first round goes back in.

25
00:02:56,182 --> 00:03:04,024
On the self-only runs, where every candidate came from the organism, the recurrence again lands much closer than assuming no change.

26
00:03:04,024 --> 00:03:07,385
And it holds up as it looks further ahead.

27
00:03:07,385 --> 00:03:13,360
Its error grows only slightly as the horizon lengthens, while assuming no change gets steadily worse.

28
00:03:13,360 --> 00:03:18,214
Selection moves a run mostly in its first rounds and then levels off.

29
00:03:18,964 --> 00:03:23,542
The deterministic forecast gives only the average path real runs scatter around.

30
00:03:23,542 --> 00:03:28,883
Each value reading comes from a limited sample of answers, so it carries noise.

31
00:03:28,883 --> 00:03:39,184
The judge's picks land around spread times agreement rather than exactly on it, training lands near but not on the kept mean, and agreement drifts between rounds.

32
00:03:39,184 --> 00:03:44,907
The stochastic version adds a random term at each point, sized from the measured residuals.

33
00:03:45,657 --> 00:03:48,563
The stochastic model reproduces the observed dynamics.

34
00:03:48,563 --> 00:03:59,358
Simulated runs accumulate about as much round-to-round value change as the real ones, change direction about as often, and scatter about as widely at the end.

35
00:03:59,358 --> 00:04:04,756
Eighty-nine percent of observed final values fall inside the model's eighty percent bands.

36
00:04:05,506 --> 00:04:07,791
Both interventions targeted spread and agreement.

37
00:04:07,791 --> 00:04:15,790
Adding base-model answers to a collapsed pool restored spread, and the judge's agreement then eroded a value that had been stuck.

38
00:04:15,790 --> 00:04:26,073
Swapping the base-model judge for a min-risk oracle, which drives agreement to its negative extreme, reversed a run that had climbed near the top of the scale.

39
00:04:26,073 --> 00:04:28,359
Each result is a single experiment.

40
00:04:29,109 --> 00:04:40,023
The training setup here is narrow: small models, short runs, filtered supervised fine-tuning, a handful of model families, and the risk-preference and insecure-code behaviors.

41
00:04:40,023 --> 00:04:51,393
Extensions should scale that up and compare this fine-tuning update with D P O, online reinforcement learning against a learned reward model, and constitutional feedback.

42
00:04:51,393 --> 00:05:01,398
The behaviors should widen to moral judgment, A I identity, and emergent misalignment, with readouts that reach past answers into internal representations.

43
00:05:01,398 --> 00:05:10,493
More open-ended setups would give models more freedom in selecting training data, revising system prompts, and editing the loop itself.

44
00:05:10,493 --> 00:05:19,134
Repeated games and agentic environments could reveal whether the dynamics favor cooperation or defection, resource grabbing, and reward hacking.

45
00:05:19,134 --> 00:05:33,232
Natural and cultural selection sculpted human values, and value dynamics research can help identify artificial selection mechanisms that can be engineered into virtuous cycles for aligning increasingly autonomous A I systems.
